  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A slow moving cold front brings the risk of shower and a few \nthunderstorms to the region through early Friday morning.\n\nNot too much of a change in the current forecast package. Still \nmonitoring a few pieces of shortwave energy ahead of a slow moving \ncold front dropping southward into the region. The front still \nremains north of the border in southeast Ontario and looks to drop \nsouth tonight into Friday morning. As a result, expect increased \ncloud cover and continued chances for showers and perhaps a few \nthunderstorms, especially heading into the afternoon and evening \nhours. \n\nThe severe weather threat appears low, but not zero given CAPE \nvalues hovering between 500-1500 j/kg and shear values around 30-40 \nkts. With a stronger piece of shortwave energy set to kick east out \nof WV expect an uptick in thunderstorm chances mainly across central \nVA and southern MD this afternoon where instability will be \nmaximized due to more sunshine. These areas could see clusters of \nthunderstorms with gusty winds as the primary threat along with \nfrequent lightning and locally heavy rainfall. Not everyone will see \nthe rain this afternoon with more scattered to widespread showers \nexpected later this evening and pre-dawn Friday morning as the front \ndrops through. Highs today will range from the mid 80s over the \nmountains to low 90s over southern MD and central VA (70S \nmountains). Lows tonight will fall back into the low to mid 60s with \nupper 60s over central VA and southern MD. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms possible on Sunday,\nfollowed by drier conditions early next week.\n\nA weak cold front meanders through the forecast area beginning \nSaturday morning into Sunday, resulting in a few possible \nthunderstorms in the afternoon into the evening. While chances for \nthese storms to be severe remain low, a few strong storms with gusty \nwinds cannot be ruled out. Otherwise, on and off showers continue \nthroughout the day Saturday and into Sunday as the front moves \nthrough.\n\nCome Monday, upper level ridging starts building over the East Coast \naccompanied by surface high pressure overhead. Primarily dry \nconditions are expected on Monday, although an isolated rain shower \ncannot be ruled out along the western portions of the forecast area \nas a cold front tracks across the Great Lakes. Precipitation chances \nincrease by midweek as another cold front tracks across the forecast \narea.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the 80s for most (70s mountains) each \nday with high humidity values yielding heat indices in the low 90s \nfor those east of the Blue Ridge. Wednesday will be the warmest day \nwith highs in the upper 80s to low 90s and heat indices in the upper \n90s.",
